# JioSaavn provider provenance

Catalog field parsing, identifier conventions, duration conversion, artwork mapping and album/artist/playlist API endpoints were inspected in `MetroFuse-main (4).zip`, `MetroFuse-main/app/src/main/kotlin/com/metrolist/music/jiosaavn/JioSaavnAudioProvider.kt`. The archive records commit `e2cca5bf2278f0345568a48adbfbfbd0046680f6`; the original source header is Metrolist Project (C) 2026, GPL-3.0. Preserve that attribution and the repository GPL license when redistributing this derived plugin.

The reusable local MetroFuse SDK prototype in `New folder (4)/plugins/jiosaavn` supplied the public-preview identity and dynamic registration approach. This implementation adapts it to the current typed MetroFuse plugin API and asynchronous isolated host HTTP interface.

Full-song locator decoding and bitrate selection are adapted from the user-supplied [JioSaavnAudioProvider.kt](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/956tris/MetroFuse/refs/heads/main/app/src/main/kotlin/com/metrolist/music/jiosaavn/JioSaavnAudioProvider.kt), inspected on 14 November 2026. The universal JavaScript module implements DES/ECB/PKCS5 using the public FIPS 46-3 tables, so the same MFP works with QuickJS on Android and Windows without native cryptography dependencies. It validates decoded CDN hosts and honors the host quality preset and bitrate ceiling, including the catalog's 320 kbps availability flag. Tests encrypt independent fixture locators using JVM javax.crypto and decode them using the production QuickJS module.

The plugin resolves explicit JioSaavn identities, prefers a full-song locator when returned by the catalog, and can use a publicly returned HTTPS JioTune preview when full audio is unavailable. The preview fallback is controlled by the persisted `previewEnabled` setting. Lossless requests and bitrate ceilings below available qualities fail explicitly. No sample rate or expiry is guessed. Preview codec, bitrate and duration remain unspecified. It supplies no ambient host cookies, authentication sessions or DRM support and does not substitute unrelated host tracks by matching.

Version 1.2.0 restores the inspected upstream provider's catalog-region routing. Without those headers, the API returned karaoke/covers for Don Toliver, The Weeknd and Travis Scott on the test network, including when the `cc` parameter was present. With the persisted `regionalCatalog` setting enabled, every catalog request includes a stable activation-scoped address from the upstream Indian allocation in `X-Forwarded-For` and `X-Real-IP`. Turning the setting off uses the network-region catalog. Song-cache entries are separated by this setting. The headers are limited to JioSaavn's `api.php`; CDN streams carry no region headers. This is provider catalog routing rather than an application language/country default.

This is an optional dynamically installed provider. Catalog and audio availability depend on the endpoint, rights and real network location. The default test suite validates deterministic parser, cryptography and quality fixtures plus actual QuickJS registration without calling JioSaavn. No live service playback result is implied by packaging or those tests. Package with `tooling/pack-mfp.ps1` and manually import `build/plugin-examples/jiosaavn.mfp` on either primary target, then enable it in Settings → Plugins.

The companion `../jiosaavn-native` implements the same provider API directly in Kotlin. Build `:plugin-jiosaavn-native:packagePluginJar` and `:plugin-jiosaavn-native:packagePluginApk` to produce `build/plugin-examples/jiosaavn.jar` and `build/plugin-examples/jiosaavn.apk` alongside the MFP. They contain compiled provider code, a native manifest and this implementation's attribution and GPL license. All formats use `metrofuse.jiosaavn`; installing another format replaces the implementation and preserves settings. The native JAR targets desktop, the native APK targets Android, and the MFP targets both.

On 14 November 2026, the opt-in production QuickJS test (`METROFUSE_JIOSAAVN_LIVE=1`) imported the packed MFP into an empty runtime, searched for Tum Hi Ho by Mithoon and Arijit Singh, resolved its full-song 320 kbps MP4 stream through the default audio pipeline, read 2,048 CDN bytes and verified the MP4 signature. It also verified Aashiqui 2 album navigation, metadata resolution, disable, reload, settings persistence and resolution after runtime restart. FFprobe measured AAC audio, 44.1 kHz stereo and 261.974 seconds; FFmpeg decoded three seconds successfully. Both deterministic and live JioSaavn tests passed. These are Windows/JVM engine and decoder checks; Android on-device playback and end-to-end app UI remain separate checks.

The same day's Western regression run passed with both packaged implementations for No Idea and After Party by Don Toliver, Blinding Lights and Starboy by The Weeknd, and goosebumps and SICKO MODE by Travis Scott. Each original was identified by title and primary artist, resolved as a full-song 320 kbps stream through the production pipeline, returned 2,048 MP4-signature bytes and passed metadata and album-membership checks. Reload, restart and persisted preview settings passed for both implementations. FFprobe identified AAC in all 12 streams; FFmpeg decoded three seconds from every returned stream. Deterministic native and QuickJS routing/quality tests and the actual native JAR loader test passed. Reports are generated under `build/jiosaavn-western-live/`. These checks exercise the Windows/JVM runtime and decoders; Android on-device and application UI playback were not run for this revision.
